## 🎯 Section 1: The Hi-Fi Bargain of the Decade

### 🚫 The Modern Desktop Audio Trap

Ask most PC enthusiasts how to upgrade their desktop sound and you will usually be pointed toward one of three expensive, compromised routes: a **£200+** wireless gaming headset, a **£600–£1,200+** commercial soundbar or AV receiver, or a tangle of active "multimedia" speakers crammed with cheap internal DSP chips.

* **OS Mixer & Resampling Overhead:** Standard desktop operating systems (Windows 10/11 or stock Linux running PipeWire/PulseAudio) funnel every note through shared software mixers that silently resample streams, apply reduced-precision digital volume attenuation, and tack on `10` to `150+ ms` of buffer and spatial-DSP lag.
* **Motherboard Electrical Noise:** Built-in motherboard sound chips sit mere millimetres from screaming GPU VRMs and high-speed PCIe lanes, picking up coil whine and ground noise while background SSD reads and shared USB interrupts introduce micro-stutters.
* **Proprietary Hardware Lock-In:** Dedicated £1,000+ network streamers, hardware synthesizers, and retro video scalers charge heavily for thick aluminium faceplates, HDMI licensing fees, proprietary apps that lose support, and locked firmware.

### ⚡ The Modular `FLAC_AX` Breakthrough

